  THE TWO RATIOS, AND WHAT EACH IS
    batch   256: naive   6.43x, lean   5.51x, and the optimization removes  14.4% of the generated side's time
    batch   512: naive  12.20x, lean  10.43x, and the optimization removes  14.5% of the generated side's time
    batch  1024: naive  36.73x, lean 110.71x, and the optimization removes -201.4% of the generated side's time
    batch  2048: naive  34.82x, lean 108.05x, and the optimization removes -210.3% of the generated side's time
  
    The naive column is the port that recomputes the flat voxel
    address from three float coordinates on every one of 32 steps.
    The lean column is the same computation with that arithmetic
    hoisted into a maintained address and precomputed strides, and
    with the per-axis bounds test computed once per ray. Both are
    plain TinyGrad: no custom_kernel, no tile size, no Context, no
    hand-unrolling, no codegen knob.
  
  WHAT TO READ
    * Both ports render the observation the hand-written kernel produced,
      on the state the hand-written kernel produced, byte for byte, on this
      card in this run. That is what makes the ratio a statement about code
      generation rather than about two different computations.
    * `naive` is minecu_tg/render.py verbatim; `lean` is the same
      computation with the index arithmetic hoisted. The difference between
      the two columns is the port's cost, not the compiler's.
    * No number here is transcribed from any notes file. Every figure is
      produced by an instrument in this notebook on this card.
    * Instruction and register counts are NOT taken from this notebook:
      they are a property of a compiler version, this image is CUDA 12.8,
      and the offline sm_75 route is 12.9 on BOTH sides.
      minecu_tg_opt/opcount.py is where the SASS numbers come from, and it
      re-derives the hand-written side's own 776 and its 38-instruction step
      from minecu_ref/build/kernels_sm_75.sass rather than quoting them.

